            "answer": "C",
            "solution": "<div class=\"solution-content\"><a name=\"526091\"></a><div itemprop=\"text\"><p>Domain: N (set of Natural numbers)</p><p>A. ∃x((x - 3) = 1) ∧ (x &lt; 3))</p><p>there is NO such x in the domain such that ((x - 3) = 1) ∧ (x &lt; 3) is true</p><p>so ∃x((x - 3) = 1) ∧ (x &lt; 3)) = <strong>False</strong></p><p>B. ∃x((x - 3) = 1) ∧ (x &gt; 5))</p><p>there is NO such x in the domain such that ((x - 3) = 1) ∧ (x &gt; 5) is true</p><p>so ∃x((x - 3) = 1) ∧ (x &gt; 5)) = <strong>False</strong></p><p>C. ∃x((x - 3) = 1) ∧ (x &gt;= 4))</p><p>take x=4 in the domain of natural numbers for which ((x - 3) = 1) ∧ (x &gt;= 4) is True</p><p>so ∃x((x - 3) = 1) ∧ (x &gt;= 4)) = <strong>True</strong></p><p>D. ∃x((x - 3) = 1) ∧ (x = 3))</p><p>there is NO such x in the domain such that ((x - 3) = 1) ∧ (x = 3) is true</p><p>so ∃x((x - 3) = 1) ∧ (x = 3)) = <strong>False</strong></p><p><strong>Answer: C</strong></p></div><br><br><small>Source: <a href=\"https://gateoverflow.in/526089/go-classes-dpp-gate-cs-discrete-mathematics-first-order-logic\" target=\"_blank\">GATE Overflow #526089</a></small></div>"
