registerTest({
    "name": "DPP 46 - Discrete Mathematics (Graph Theory - Part 7)",
    "testLabel": "DPP 46",
    "testType": "DPP",
    "bracket": "Graph Theory - Part 7",
    "subject": "dm",
    "series": "cse-dpp-2027",
    "date": "Nov 03, 2026",
    "duration": "30 Min",
    "totalMarks": 10,
    "topicsCovered": "Graph Theory (Practice Set 7)",
    "questions": [
        {
            "marks": 1,
            "neg": 0.33,
            "type": "MCQ",
            "text": "<span style=\"display: inline;\"><div itemprop=\"text\"><p>In a graph, the degree of a vertex is the number of edges incident (connected) on it. Which of the following is true for every graph $G$ ?</p></div></span>",
            "image": "",
            "options": [
                "<span style=\"display: inline;\">There are even number of vertices of even degree.<br/></span>",
                "<span style=\"display: inline;\">There are odd number of vertices of even degree.<br/></span>",
                "<span style=\"display: inline;\">There are even number of vertices of odd degree.<br/></span>",
                "<span style=\"display: inline;\">There are odd number of vertices of odd degree.</span>"
            ],
            "answer": "C",
            "solution": "<div class=\"solution-content\"><a name=\"475871\"></a><div itemprop=\"text\">Option C Apply Handshaking Theorem<br/> <br/> </div><br><br><small>Source: <a href=\"https://gateoverflow.in/475856/go-classes-dpp-discrete-mathematics-graph-theory-tifr\" target=\"_blank\">GATE Overflow #475856</a></small></div>"
        },
        {
            "marks": 1,
            "neg": 0.33,
            "type": "MCQ",
            "text": "<span style=\"display: inline;\"><div itemprop=\"text\"><p>Which of the following graphs are bipartite$?$<br/><img alt=\"\" src=\"https://gateoverflow.in/?qa=blob&amp;qa_blobid=6093145347459224121\"/></p></div></span>",
            "image": "https://gateoverflow.in/?qa=blob&qa_blobid=6093145347459224121",
            "options": [
                "<span style=\"display: inline;\">Only ($1$)<br/></span>",
                "<span style=\"display: inline;\">Only ($2$)<br/></span>",
                "<span style=\"display: inline;\">Only ($2$) and ($3$)<br/></span>",
                "<span style=\"display: inline;\">None of ($1$), ($2$), ($3$)</span>"
            ],
            "answer": "A",
            "solution": "<div class=\"solution-content\"><a name=\"475855\"></a><div itemprop=\"text\"><ul><li>A graph is said to be a bipartite graph, when vertices of that graph can be divided into two independent sets such that every edge in the graph is either start from the first set and ended in the second set connected to the first set, in other words, we can say that no edge can found in the same set.</li><li>Bipartite graphs are precisely the class of graphs that are $2$ -colorable. Checking of a bipartite graph is possible by using the vertex coloring. When a vertex is in the same set, it has the same color, for another set, the color will change.<br/><br/><img alt=\"\" src=\"https://gateoverflow.in/?qa=blob&amp;qa_blobid=8776371009253589782\"/>For the given option we can see only option $2$) is $2$-colorable graph.<br/>Moreover, a bipartite graph has no odd length cycle which is not present in option $2$)<br/>Hence Option $2$) is correct answer</li></ul></div><br><br><small>Source: <a href=\"https://gateoverflow.in/475854/go-classes-dpp-discrete-mathematics-graph-theory-tifr\" target=\"_blank\">GATE Overflow #475854</a></small></div>"
        },
        {
            "marks": 1,
            "neg": 0.33,
            "type": "MCQ",
            "text": "<span style=\"display: inline;\"><div itemprop=\"text\"><p>How many of the following statements are correct?<br/>1. All cyclic graphs are complete graphs.<br/>2. All complete graphs are cyclic graphs.<br/>3. All paths are bipartite.<br/>4. All cyclic graphs are bipartite.<br/>5. There are cyclic graphs which are complete.</p></div></span>",
            "image": "",
            "options": [
                "<span style=\"display: inline;\">$1$</span>",
                "<span style=\"display: inline;\">$2$</span>",
                "<span style=\"display: inline;\">$3$</span>",
                "<span style=\"display: inline;\">$4$</span>"
            ],
            "answer": "B",
            "solution": "<div class=\"solution-content\"><a name=\"473673\"></a><div itemprop=\"text\">1. All cyclic graphs are complete graphs.<br/> False. A cyclic graph only needs to contain a cycle. It does not require all possible edges between vertices, unlike a complete graph. For example, a simple cycle $C_4$ is not complete.<br/> <br/> 2. All complete graphs are cyclic graphs.<br/> False. A complete graph with 1 or 2 vertices ( $K_1, K_2$ ) does not contain any cycle. Only complete graphs with 3 or more vertices are cyclic.<br/> <br/> 3. All paths are bipartite.<br/> True. Path graphs are acyclic and all acyclic graphs are bipartite. (A graph is bipartite if and only if it doesn't contain any odd length cycle)<br/> <br/> 4. All cyclic graphs are bipartite.<br/> False. Cycles of odd length (like $C_3, C_5$ ) are not bipartite, as they cannot be 2-colored without adjacent vertices sharing a color.<br/> <br/> 5. There are cyclic graphs which are complete.<br/> True. $C_3$ is a cycle and also a complete graph ( $K_3$ ).<br/> <br/> <br/> Number of correct statements: 2<br/> Correct option: (b) 2</div><br><br><small>Source: <a href=\"https://gateoverflow.in/473672/go-classes-dpp-graph-theory-types-of-graphs-university-of-calicut\" target=\"_blank\">GATE Overflow #473672</a></small></div>"
        },
        {
            "marks": 1,
            "neg": 0.33,
            "type": "MCQ",
            "text": "<span style=\"display: inline;\"><div itemprop=\"text\"><p>If G is the forest with 54 vertices and 17 connected components, then number of edges in G is ?</p></div></span>",
            "image": "",
            "options": [
                "<span style=\"display: inline;\">$37$</span>",
                "<span style=\"display: inline;\">$71$</span>",
                "<span style=\"display: inline;\">$17$</span>",
                "<span style=\"display: inline;\">$54$</span>"
            ],
            "answer": "A",
            "solution": "<div class=\"solution-content\"><a name=\"473929\"></a><div itemprop=\"text\">A forest is a collection of trees, i.e., connected acyclic components.<br/> For a tree with $v_i$ vertices, it has exactly $v_i-1$ edges.<br/> Let the forest have:<br/> - $v$ total vertices<br/> - $k$ connected components (i.e., $k$ trees)<br/> - The $i^{\\text {th }}$ tree has $v_i$ vertices<br/> <br/> So,<br/> <br/> $$<br/> v_1+v_2+\\cdots+v_k=v<br/> $$<br/> <br/> Each component contributes $v_i-1$ edges, so total number of edges:<br/> <br/> $$<br/> \\left(v_1-1\\right)+\\left(v_2-1\\right)+\\cdots+\\left(v_k-1\\right)=\\left(v_1+v_2+\\cdots+v_k\\right)-k=v-k<br/> $$<br/> <br/> Given: $v=54, k=17$<br/> Total edges $=54-17=37$</div><br><br><small>Source: <a href=\"https://gateoverflow.in/473670/go-classes-dpp-graph-theory-acyclic-graphs-university-of-calicut\" target=\"_blank\">GATE Overflow #473670</a></small></div>"
        },
        {
            "marks": 1,
            "neg": 0.33,
            "type": "MCQ",
            "text": "<span style=\"display: inline;\"><div itemprop=\"text\"><p>For a given graph $G$ having $v$ vertices and $e$ edges which is connected and has no cycles, which of the following statements is true?</p></div></span>",
            "image": "",
            "options": [
                "<span style=\"display: inline;\">$v=e$</span>",
                "<span style=\"display: inline;\">$v=e+1$</span>",
                "<span style=\"display: inline;\">$v+1=e$</span>",
                "<span style=\"display: inline;\">$v=e-1$</span>"
            ],
            "answer": "B",
            "solution": "<div class=\"solution-content\"><a name=\"473927\"></a><div itemprop=\"text\">The graph is:<br/> - Connected<br/> - Has no cycles<br/> <br/> This is the definition of a tree.<br/> For any tree with $v$ vertices, the number of edges $e$ is:<br/> <br/> $$<br/> e=v-1 \\Rightarrow v=e+1<br/> $$<br/> <br/> So, option (b) $e+1=v$ is correct.</div><br><br><small>Source: <a href=\"https://gateoverflow.in/473668/go-classes-dpp-graph-theory-acyclic-graphs-university-of-calicut\" target=\"_blank\">GATE Overflow #473668</a></small></div>"
        },
        {
            "marks": 1,
            "neg": 0.33,
            "type": "MCQ",
            "text": "<span style=\"display: inline;\"><div itemprop=\"text\"><p>Which of the following is true for any simple connected graph with more than 2 vertices.</p></div></span>",
            "image": "",
            "options": [
                "<span style=\"display: inline;\">No two vertices have same degree</span>",
                "<span style=\"display: inline;\">At least two vertices have same degree</span>",
                "<span style=\"display: inline;\">At least 3 vertices have same degree</span>",
                "<span style=\"display: inline;\">All vertices have same degree</span>"
            ],
            "answer": "B",
            "solution": "<div class=\"solution-content\"><a name=\"473928\"></a><div itemprop=\"text\">Correct Answer: (B) At least two vertices have same degree<br/> <br/> Proof (using the Pigeonhole Principle):<br/> In a simple graph with $\\mathrm{n}&gt;2$ vertices:<br/> - Each vertex can have a degree from $\\mathbf{1}$ to $\\mathbf{n - 1}$ (since the graph is connected, degree 0 is not possible).<br/> - But among these $\\mathbf{n}$ vertices, you cannot have one vertex with degree 0 and another with degree $\\mathbf{n - 1}$ (since degree $n-1$ implies it's connected to all other $n-1$ vertices, and thus no one can have degree 0).<br/> <br/> So, the possible degrees are limited to either:<br/> - 0 to $n-2$, or<br/> - 1 to $n-1 \\rightarrow$ i.e., only $n-1$ possible degree values.<br/> <br/> Hence, by the Pigeonhole Principle, at least two vertices must have the same degree.<br/> <br/> <br/> <br/> </div><br><br><small>Source: <a href=\"https://gateoverflow.in/473666/go-classes-dpp-graph-theory-connected-graphs-university-of-calicut\" target=\"_blank\">GATE Overflow #473666</a></small></div>"
        },
        {
            "marks": 1,
            "neg": 0.33,
            "type": "MCQ",
            "text": "<span style=\"display: inline;\"><div itemprop=\"text\"><p>What is the maximum number of edges in a bipartite graph having 12 vertices.</p></div></span>",
            "image": "",
            "options": [
                "<span style=\"display: inline;\">$24$</span>",
                "<span style=\"display: inline;\">$38$</span>",
                "<span style=\"display: inline;\">$36$</span>",
                "<span style=\"display: inline;\">$32$</span>"
            ],
            "answer": "A",
            "solution": "<div class=\"solution-content\"><a name=\"473922\"></a><div itemprop=\"text\">To maximize the number of edges in a bipartite graph with 12 vertices, split them into two sets of 6 and 6 (most balanced):<br/> <br/> $$<br/> \\text { Max edges }=6 \\times 6=36<br/> $$<br/> </div><br><br><small>Source: <a href=\"https://gateoverflow.in/473664/go-classes-dpp-graph-theory-bi-partite-graphs-university-of-calicut\" target=\"_blank\">GATE Overflow #473664</a></small></div>"
        },
        {
            "marks": 1,
            "neg": 0,
            "type": "NAT",
            "text": "<span style=\"display: inline;\"><div itemprop=\"text\"><p><img alt=\"\" src=\"https://gateoverflow.in/?qa=blob&amp;qa_blobid=3976083327636399930\"/></p><p> </p><p>How many of the following given set of vertices is/are CORRECT strongly connected components for the given directed graph?</p></div></span>",
            "image": "https://gateoverflow.in/?qa=blob&qa_blobid=3976083327636399930",
            "options": [
                "<span style=\"display: inline;\">$C$</span>",
                "<span style=\"display: inline;\">$A$</span>",
                "<span style=\"display: inline;\">$E$</span>",
                "<span style=\"display: inline;\">$IC$</span>",
                "<span style=\"display: inline;\">$F$</span>",
                "<span style=\"display: inline;\">$IDE$</span>",
                "<span style=\"display: inline;\">$I$</span>",
                "<span style=\"display: inline;\">$H$</span>",
                "<span style=\"display: inline;\">$GJ$</span>"
            ],
            "answer": "5",
            "solution": "<div class=\"solution-content\"><a name=\"499014\"></a><div itemprop=\"text\"><p>Answer:<span style=\"color:#e67e22;\"><strong> 5</strong></span> from above given are strongly connected components.<br/> </p><blockquote><p>Apart from answer: total 6 strongly connected components are present in given graph. [(ABDH), (GJ), C, I, E, F]</p></blockquote></div><br><br><small>Source: <a href=\"https://gateoverflow.in/471878/go-classes-dpp-graph-theory-graph-connectivity-university-of-california-berkeley\" target=\"_blank\">GATE Overflow #471878</a></small></div>"
        },
        {
            "marks": 1,
            "neg": 0.33,
            "type": "MCQ",
            "text": "<span style=\"display: inline;\"><div itemprop=\"text\"><p>Is it possible to draw 9 rectangles in the plane such that each of them intersects either 1 or 3 other rectangles?</p></div></span>",
            "image": "",
            "options": [
                "<span style=\"display: inline;\">Yes</span>",
                "<span style=\"display: inline;\">No</span>"
            ],
            "answer": "A",
            "solution": "<div class=\"solution-content\"><a name=\"472066\"></a><div itemprop=\"text\">treat rectangles as vertices . two traiangles intersect means they ( vertices ) have edge between them . <br/> <br/> so each of the 9 vertices has 1 or 3 degree but this is not possible as in simple graph as we know that the number of odd degree vertices are even .<br/> whatever case you take we cannot make both 1 degree and 3 degree vertices even in number .<br/> <br/> hence the statement is false</div><br><br><small>Source: <a href=\"https://gateoverflow.in/471876/go-classes-dpp-graph-theory-counting-e%C3%B6tv%C3%B6s-lor%C3%A1nd-university\" target=\"_blank\">GATE Overflow #471876</a></small></div>"
        },
        {
            "marks": 1,
            "neg": 0.33,
            "type": "MCQ",
            "text": "<span style=\"display: inline;\"><div itemprop=\"text\"><p>Is the graph below planar?<br/><img alt=\"\" src=\"https://gateoverflow.in/?qa=blob&amp;qa_blobid=5559450088428549646\"/></p></div></span>",
            "image": "https://gateoverflow.in/?qa=blob&qa_blobid=5559450088428549646",
            "options": [
                "<span style=\"display: inline;\">True</span>",
                "<span style=\"display: inline;\">False</span>"
            ],
            "answer": "A",
            "solution": "<div class=\"solution-content\"><a name=\"472087\"></a><div itemprop=\"text\"><p>B.False</p><p>In the given graph we can't draw the edge from A-E without intersecting the edge.</p><p>Handwritten explanation is given below...<img alt=\"\" src=\"https://gateoverflow.in/?qa=blob&amp;qa_blobid=5961864630209969042\"/></p></div><br><br><small>Source: <a href=\"https://gateoverflow.in/471874/go-classes-dpp-graph-theory-graph-planarity-e%C3%B6tv%C3%B6s-lor%C3%A1nd-university\" target=\"_blank\">GATE Overflow #471874</a></small></div>"
        }
    ]
});

