registerTest({
    "name": "DPP 48 - Discrete Mathematics (Group Theory & Abstract Algebra - Part 1)",
    "testLabel": "DPP 48",
    "testType": "DPP",
    "bracket": "Group Theory & Abstract Algebra - Part 1",
    "subject": "dm",
    "series": "cse-dpp-2027",
    "date": "Nov 03, 2026",
    "duration": "30 Min",
    "totalMarks": 10,
    "topicsCovered": "Group Theory & Abstract Algebra (Practice Set 1)",
    "questions": [
        {
            "marks": 1,
            "neg": 0,
            "type": "NAT",
            "text": "<span style=\"display: inline;\"><div itemprop=\"text\">Eight couples are invited to a party. A group of exactly $10$ people is to be selected. The rule is that if a husband is selected, then his wife must also be selected. However, a wife may be selected without her husband. Also, a particular wife $W_1$ must be selected, and at least $3$ husbands must be selected. How many different groups are possible?</div></span>",
            "image": "",
            "options": [],
            "answer": "595",
            "solution": "<div class=\"solution-content\"><a name=\"530772\"></a><div itemprop=\"text\"><p data-path-to-node=\"0\">To find the total number of possible groups, we can break the problem down into two main cases based on whether $W_1$'s husband, $H_1$, is selected.</p><p data-path-to-node=\"1\">Because of the rules, choosing a husband automatically forces his wife into the group (occupying $2$ spots total), but choosing a wife without her husband only occupies $1$ spot.</p><p data-path-to-node=\"2\"><b data-index-in-node=\"0\" data-path-to-node=\"2\">Case </b>$\\mathbf{1}:$<b data-index-in-node=\"0\" data-path-to-node=\"2\"> $H_1$ is selected</b></p><p data-path-to-node=\"3\">If $H_1$ is selected, $W_1$ is also selected.</p><ul data-path-to-node=\"4\"><li><p data-path-to-node=\"4,0,0\"><b data-index-in-node=\"0\" data-path-to-node=\"4,0,0\">People currently in the group:</b> $2$ ($H_1$ and $W_1$)</p></li><li><p data-path-to-node=\"4,1,0\"><b data-index-in-node=\"0\" data-path-to-node=\"4,1,0\">People still needed:</b> $8$ (chosen from the remaining $7$ couples)</p></li><li><p data-path-to-node=\"4,2,0\"><b data-index-in-node=\"0\" data-path-to-node=\"4,2,0\">Husbands still needed:</b> At least $2$ (since $H_1$ counts as $1$, and we need at least $3$)</p></li></ul><p data-path-to-node=\"5\">Let $j$ be the number of additional husbands selected from the remaining $7$ couples. Selecting $j$ husbands automatically adds their $j$ wives, taking up $2j$ spots. The remaining $8 - 2j$ spots must be filled by wives whose husbands were <i data-index-in-node=\"228\" data-path-to-node=\"5\">not</i> selected.</p><ul data-path-to-node=\"6\"><li><p data-path-to-node=\"6,0,0\"><b data-index-in-node=\"0\" data-path-to-node=\"6,0,0\">If $j = 2$ (</b>$\\mathbf{3}$<b data-index-in-node=\"0\" data-path-to-node=\"6,0,0\"> husbands total):</b></p><ul data-path-to-node=\"6,0,1\"><li><p data-path-to-node=\"6,0,1,0,0\">Choose $2$ husbands from $7$ couples: $\\binom{7}{2} = 21$ ways.</p></li><li><p data-path-to-node=\"6,0,1,1,0\">Choose $8 - 2(2) = 4$ wives from the remaining $5$ couples: $\\binom{5}{4} = 5$ ways.</p></li><li><p data-path-to-node=\"6,0,1,2,0\"><b data-index-in-node=\"0\" data-path-to-node=\"6,0,1,2,0\">Combinations:</b> $21 \\times 5 = \\mathbf{105}$</p></li></ul></li><li><p data-path-to-node=\"6,1,0\"><b data-index-in-node=\"0\" data-path-to-node=\"6,1,0\">If $j = 3$ (</b>$\\mathbf{4}$<b data-index-in-node=\"0\" data-path-to-node=\"6,1,0\"> husbands total):</b></p><ul data-path-to-node=\"6,1,1\"><li><p data-path-to-node=\"6,1,1,0,0\">Choose $3$ husbands from $7$ couples: $\\binom{7}{3} = 35$ ways.</p></li><li><p data-path-to-node=\"6,1,1,1,0\">Choose $8 - 2(3) = 2$ wives from the remaining $4$ couples: $\\binom{4}{2} = 6$ ways.</p></li><li><p data-path-to-node=\"6,1,1,2,0\"><b data-index-in-node=\"0\" data-path-to-node=\"6,1,1,2,0\">Combinations:</b> $35 times 6 = \\mathbf{210}$</p></li></ul></li><li><p data-path-to-node=\"6,2,0\"><b data-index-in-node=\"0\" data-path-to-node=\"6,2,0\">If $j = 4$ (</b>$\\mathbf{5}$<b data-index-in-node=\"0\" data-path-to-node=\"6,2,0\"> husbands total):</b></p><ul data-path-to-node=\"6,2,1\"><li><p data-path-to-node=\"6,2,1,0,0\">Choose $4$ husbands from $7$ couples: $\\binom{7}{4} = 35$ ways.</p></li><li><p data-path-to-node=\"6,2,1,1,0\">Choose $8 - 2(4) = 0$ wives from the remaining $3$ couples: $\\binom{3}{0} = 1$ way.</p></li><li><p data-path-to-node=\"6,2,1,2,0\"><b data-index-in-node=\"0\" data-path-to-node=\"6,2,1,2,0\">Combinations:</b> $35 \\times 1 = \\mathbf{35}$</p></li></ul></li></ul><p data-path-to-node=\"7\"><i data-index-in-node=\"0\" data-path-to-node=\"7\">(Note: We cannot select $j = 5$ because that would require $10$ spots, and we only have $8$ remaining).</i></p><p data-path-to-node=\"8\"><b data-index-in-node=\"0\" data-path-to-node=\"8\">Total for Case </b>$\\mathbf{1}: 105 + 210 + 35 = \\mathbf{350}$<b data-index-in-node=\"35\" data-path-to-node=\"8\"> possible groups</b></p><p data-path-to-node=\"10\"><br/><b data-index-in-node=\"0\" data-path-to-node=\"10\">Case </b>$\\mathbf{2}:$<b data-index-in-node=\"0\" data-path-to-node=\"10\"> $H_1$ is NOT selected</b></p><p data-path-to-node=\"11\">Because the rules dictate $W_1$ must be in the group, she is selected without her husband.</p><ul data-path-to-node=\"12\"><li><p data-path-to-node=\"12,0,0\"><b data-index-in-node=\"0\" data-path-to-node=\"12,0,0\">People currently in the group:</b> $1$ ($W_1$)</p></li><li><p data-path-to-node=\"12,1,0\"><b data-index-in-node=\"0\" data-path-to-node=\"12,1,0\">People still needed:</b> $9$ (chosen from the remaining $7$ couples)</p></li><li><p data-path-to-node=\"12,2,0\"><b data-index-in-node=\"0\" data-path-to-node=\"12,2,0\">Husbands still needed:</b> At least $3$</p></li></ul><p data-path-to-node=\"13\">Let $j$ be the number of husbands selected from the remaining $7$ couples. Selecting $j$ husbands automatically adds their $j$ wives, taking up $2j$ spots. The remaining $9 - 2j$ spots must be filled by wives whose husbands were <i data-index-in-node=\"217\" data-path-to-node=\"13\">not</i> selected.</p><ul data-path-to-node=\"14\"><li><p data-path-to-node=\"14,0,0\"><b data-index-in-node=\"0\" data-path-to-node=\"14,0,0\">If $j = 3$ (</b>$\\mathbf{3}$<b data-index-in-node=\"0\" data-path-to-node=\"14,0,0\"> husbands total):</b></p><ul data-path-to-node=\"14,0,1\"><li><p data-path-to-node=\"14,0,1,0,0\">Choose $3$ husbands from $7$ couples: $\\binom{7}{3} = 35$ ways.</p></li><li><p data-path-to-node=\"14,0,1,1,0\">Choose $9 - 2(3) = 3$ wives from the remaining $4$ couples: $\\binom{4}{3} = 4$ ways.</p></li><li><p data-path-to-node=\"14,0,1,2,0\"><b data-index-in-node=\"0\" data-path-to-node=\"14,0,1,2,0\">Combinations:</b> $35 \\times 4 = \\mathbf{140}$</p></li></ul></li><li><p data-path-to-node=\"14,1,0\"><b data-index-in-node=\"0\" data-path-to-node=\"14,1,0\">If $j = 4$ (</b>$\\mathbf{4}$<b data-index-in-node=\"0\" data-path-to-node=\"14,1,0\"> husbands total):</b></p><ul data-path-to-node=\"14,1,1\"><li><p data-path-to-node=\"14,1,1,0,0\">Choose $4$ husbands from $7$ couples: $\\binom{7}{4} = 35$ ways.</p></li><li><p data-path-to-node=\"14,1,1,1,0\">Choose $9 - 2(4) = 1$ wife from the remaining $3$ couples: $\\binom{3}{1} = 3$ ways.</p></li><li><p data-path-to-node=\"14,1,1,2,0\"><b data-index-in-node=\"0\" data-path-to-node=\"14,1,1,2,0\">Combinations:</b> $35 × 3 = \\mathbf{105}$</p></li></ul></li></ul><p data-path-to-node=\"15\"><i data-index-in-node=\"0\" data-path-to-node=\"15\">(Note: We cannot select $j = 5$ because that would require $10$ spots, and we only have $9$ remaining).</i></p><p data-path-to-node=\"16\"><b data-index-in-node=\"0\" data-path-to-node=\"16\">Total for Case </b>$\\mathbf{2}: 140 + 105 = \\mathbf{245}$<b data-index-in-node=\"30\" data-path-to-node=\"16\"> possible groups</b></p><p data-path-to-node=\"18\"><br/><b data-index-in-node=\"0\" data-path-to-node=\"18\">Final Calculation :</b></p><p data-path-to-node=\"19\">To find the total number of different groups possible, we add the valid combinations from both cases together:</p><p>$$350 + 245 = \\boxed{595}$$</p><p data-path-to-node=\"21\">There are $\\mathbf{595}$ different groups possible.</p></div><br><br><small>Source: <a href=\"https://gateoverflow.in/530771/go-classes-dpp-gate-cs-discrete-mathematics-combinatorics\" target=\"_blank\">GATE Overflow #530771</a></small></div>"
        },
        {
            "marks": 1,
            "neg": 0.33,
            "type": "MCQ",
            "text": "<span style=\"display: inline;\"><div itemprop=\"text\"><p>The 600 students at King Middle School are divided into three groups of equal size for lunch. Each group has lunch at a different time. A computer randomly assigns each student to one of three lunch groups. The probability that three friends, Al , Bob, and Carol, will be assigned to the same lunch group is approximately</p></div></span>",
            "image": "",
            "options": [
                "<span style=\"display: inline;\">$\\frac{1}{27}$</span>",
                "<span style=\"display: inline;\">$\\frac{1}{9}$</span>",
                "<span style=\"display: inline;\">$\\frac{1}{8}$</span>",
                "<span style=\"display: inline;\">$\\frac{1}{6}$</span>"
            ],
            "answer": "A",
            "solution": "<div class=\"solution-content\"><a name=\"529163\"></a><div itemprop=\"text\">Let us first assign Al to a group. We want to estimate the probability that Bob and Carol are assigned to the same group as Al. As the groups are large and of equal size, we can estimate that Bob and Carol each have $\\mathrm{a} \\approx \\frac{1}{3}$ probability of being assigned to the same group as AI, and that these events are mostly independent of each other. The probability that all three are in the same lunch group is approximately $\\left(\\frac{1}{3}\\right)^2=\\frac{1}{9}$, or<br/> (B).</div><br><br><small>Source: <a href=\"https://gateoverflow.in/529162/go-classes-dpp-gate-cs-discrete-mathematics-combinatorics\" target=\"_blank\">GATE Overflow #529162</a></small></div>"
        },
        {
            "marks": 1,
            "neg": 0.33,
            "type": "MCQ",
            "text": "<span style=\"display: inline;\"><div itemprop=\"text\"><p>If ( G ) is a group and ( H ) is a subset of ( G ) such that ( H ) is closed under the group operation and every element in ( H ) has an inverse in ( H ), which statement about ( H ) is true?</p></div></span>",
            "image": "",
            "options": [
                "<span style=\"display: inline;\">( H ) is necessarily a subgroup of ( G ).</span>",
                "<span style=\"display: inline;\">( H ) must contain the identity element of ( G ).</span>",
                "<span style=\"display: inline;\">( H ) is a cyclic group.</span>",
                "<span style=\"display: inline;\">( H ) is necessarily the entire group ( G ).</span>"
            ],
            "answer": "A",
            "solution": "<div class=\"solution-content\"><a name=\"529116\"></a><div itemprop=\"text\"><p>It is given G is group and H is subset closed under G,<br/>lets take element <span class=\"base\"><span class=\"mord mathnormal\">a</span><span class=\"mrel\">∈</span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"mord mathnormal\">H,</span></span> its given <span class=\"base\"><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"mord mathnormal\">a</span><span class=\"msupsub\"><span class=\"vlist-t\"><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\"><span><span class=\"sizing reset-size6 size3 mtight\"><span class=\"mord mtight\"><span class=\"mord mtight\">^−</span><span class=\"mord mtight\">1(inverse)</span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span><span class=\"mrel\">∈</span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\">H ,since closed under G therefore a* a^-1=e </span><span class=\"mrel\">∈ H<br/>That means H is subgroup!</span></span></p></div><br><br><small>Source: <a href=\"https://gateoverflow.in/529071/go-classes-dpp-gate-cs-discrete-mathematics-group-theory\" target=\"_blank\">GATE Overflow #529071</a></small></div>"
        },
        {
            "marks": 1,
            "neg": 0.33,
            "type": "MCQ",
            "text": "<span style=\"display: inline;\"><div itemprop=\"text\"><p>Every group of order 4 is</p></div></span>",
            "image": "",
            "options": [
                "<span style=\"display: inline;\">Cyclic</span>",
                "<span style=\"display: inline;\">Abelian</span>",
                "<span style=\"display: inline;\">Non abelian</span>",
                "<span style=\"display: inline;\">None of the Above</span>"
            ],
            "answer": "B",
            "solution": "<div class=\"solution-content\"><a name=\"529118\"></a><div itemprop=\"text\">Smallest noncyclic group has order 4 so we cant comment about cyclicity but every group of order &lt;=4 is abelian so option B</div><br><br><small>Source: <a href=\"https://gateoverflow.in/529066/go-classes-dpp-gate-cs-discrete-mathematics-group-theory\" target=\"_blank\">GATE Overflow #529066</a></small></div>"
        },
        {
            "marks": 1,
            "neg": 0.33,
            "type": "MCQ",
            "text": "<span style=\"display: inline;\"><div itemprop=\"text\"><p>The inverse of -1 in the multiplicative group, $\\{1,-1, i,-i\\}$ is</p></div></span>",
            "image": "",
            "options": [
                "<span style=\"display: inline;\">1</span>",
                "<span style=\"display: inline;\">-1</span>",
                "<span style=\"display: inline;\">i</span>",
                "<span style=\"display: inline;\">-i</span>"
            ],
            "answer": "B",
            "solution": "<div class=\"solution-content\"><a name=\"530312\"></a><div itemprop=\"text\"><ul><li><p data-path-to-node=\"7,1,1,0,0\">$1^{-1} = 1$</p></li><li><p data-path-to-node=\"7,1,1,1,0\">$(-1)^{-1} = -1$</p></li><li><p data-path-to-node=\"7,1,1,2,0\">$i^{-1} = -i$ (since $i \\cdot -i = -i^2 = -(-1) = 1$)</p></li><li><p data-path-to-node=\"7,1,1,3,0\">$(-i)^{-1} = i$</p></li></ul><div>Hence, <strong>option</strong> <strong>B</strong></div></div><br><br><small>Source: <a href=\"https://gateoverflow.in/529062/go-classes-dpp-gate-cs-discrete-mathematics-group-theory\" target=\"_blank\">GATE Overflow #529062</a></small></div>"
        },
        {
            "marks": 1,
            "neg": 0.33,
            "type": "MCQ",
            "text": "<span style=\"display: inline;\"><div itemprop=\"text\"><p>Let $G=\\{1,i,-i,-1\\}$, where $i^2=-1$, under the operation of complex multiplication.</p><p>Which of the following statements is correct?</p></div></span>",
            "image": "",
            "options": [
                "<span style=\"display: inline;\">$G$ is a semigroup but not a monoid.<br/></span>",
                "<span style=\"display: inline;\">$G$ is an abelian group but not a cyclic group.<br/></span>",
                "<span style=\"display: inline;\">$G$ is a cyclic group of order $4$.<br/></span>",
                "<span style=\"display: inline;\">$G$ is a non-abelian group.</span>"
            ],
            "answer": "C",
            "solution": "<div class=\"solution-content\"><a name=\"529119\"></a><div itemprop=\"text\">Cyclic group need to have atleast 1 generator and in given group i,-i are 2 generators because others can be expressed as power of this two.hence option C</div><br><br><small>Source: <a href=\"https://gateoverflow.in/529051/go-classes-dpp-gate-cs-discrete-mathematics-group-theory\" target=\"_blank\">GATE Overflow #529051</a></small></div>"
        },
        {
            "marks": 1,
            "neg": 0.33,
            "type": "MCQ",
            "text": "<span style=\"display: inline;\"><div itemprop=\"text\"><p>Identity element of the group $R^*$ under Multiplication is</p></div></span>",
            "image": "",
            "options": [
                "<span style=\"display: inline;\">1</span>",
                "<span style=\"display: inline;\">2</span>",
                "<span style=\"display: inline;\">0</span>",
                "<span style=\"display: inline;\">-1</span>"
            ],
            "answer": "A",
            "solution": "<div class=\"solution-content\"><a name=\"530315\"></a><div itemprop=\"text\"><p>R* = set of all <b data-index-in-node=\"67\" data-path-to-node=\"2,1,0\">non-zero real numbers</b>.<br/><br/>x * 1= x<br/><br/>Therefore, <strong>option A</strong></p></div><br><br><small>Source: <a href=\"https://gateoverflow.in/529041/go-classes-dpp-gate-cs-discrete-mathematics-group-theory\" target=\"_blank\">GATE Overflow #529041</a></small></div>"
        },
        {
            "marks": 1,
            "neg": 0.33,
            "type": "MCQ",
            "text": "<span style=\"display: inline;\"><div itemprop=\"text\"><p>Identity element of the group $R$ under addition is</p></div></span>",
            "image": "",
            "options": [
                "<span style=\"display: inline;\">1</span>",
                "<span style=\"display: inline;\">2</span>",
                "<span style=\"display: inline;\">0</span>",
                "<span style=\"display: inline;\">-1</span>"
            ],
            "answer": "C",
            "solution": "<div class=\"solution-content\"><a name=\"528991\"></a><div itemprop=\"text\"><p>operation Given is (Addition) </p><p>For indetity element a + e = a .</p><p>now subtract a from both side </p><p> a + e -a = a-a </p><p>e = 0 </p><p>or we can also do it with putting option value</p><p>put e = 0 </p><p>a + 0 = a </p><p>or 5 + 0 = 5</p><p>so option c is correct.</p><p><strong>Note : if Operation that is given is multiplication.</strong></p><p>then a * e = a </p><p>a * 1 = 1 would be answer </p><p> </p></div><br><br><small>Source: <a href=\"https://gateoverflow.in/528978/go-classes-dpp-gate-cs-discrete-mathematics-group-theory\" target=\"_blank\">GATE Overflow #528978</a></small></div>"
        },
        {
            "marks": 1,
            "neg": 0.33,
            "type": "MCQ",
            "text": "<span style=\"display: inline;\"><div itemprop=\"text\"><p>A subgroup $H$ of a group $G$ is normal if:</p></div></span>",
            "image": "",
            "options": [
                "<span style=\"display: inline;\">$a H=H a$ for all $a \\in G$.</span>",
                "<span style=\"display: inline;\">$H$ is commutative.</span>",
                "<span style=\"display: inline;\">$H$ has the same order as $G$.</span>",
                "<span style=\"display: inline;\">$H$ is the center of $G$.</span>"
            ],
            "answer": "A",
            "solution": "<div class=\"solution-content\"><a name=\"533966\"></a><div itemprop=\"text\"><p data-path-to-node=\"1\">A subgroup $H$ of a group $G$ is <b data-index-in-node=\"39\" data-path-to-node=\"1\">normal</b> if <b data-index-in-node=\"0\" data-path-to-node=\"2,0,0\">Left and Right Cosets are Equal:</b> $aH = Ha$. The set formed by multiplying $H$ on the left by $a$ is identical to the set formed by multiplying $H$ on the right by $a$.</p><p data-path-to-node=\"1\">=&gt; $aH = Ha$</p><p data-path-to-node=\"1\">=&gt; $ \\{ah_1, ah_2, ah_3, \\dots\\} = \\{h_1a, h_2a, h_3a, \\dots\\}$</p><p data-path-to-node=\"1\"> </p></div><br><br><small>Source: <a href=\"https://gateoverflow.in/528977/go-classes-dpp-gate-cs-discrete-mathematics-group-theory\" target=\"_blank\">GATE Overflow #528977</a></small></div>"
        },
        {
            "marks": 1,
            "neg": 0.33,
            "type": "MCQ",
            "text": "<span style=\"display: inline;\"><div itemprop=\"text\"><p>In a group $G$, an element a has order 2 if:</p></div></span>",
            "image": "",
            "options": [
                "<span style=\"display: inline;\">$a^2=a$.</span>",
                "<span style=\"display: inline;\">$a^2=e$.</span>",
                "<span style=\"display: inline;\">$a=e$.</span>",
                "<span style=\"display: inline;\">none of these.</span>"
            ],
            "answer": "B",
            "solution": "<div class=\"solution-content\"><a name=\"528998\"></a><div itemprop=\"text\"><ul><li><p data-path-to-node=\"10,0,0\"><b data-index-in-node=\"0\" data-path-to-node=\"10,0,0\">$a^2 = a$</b>: This describes an <b data-index-in-node=\"27\" data-path-to-node=\"10,0,0\">idempotent</b> element. In any group, the only idempotent element is the identity ($e$), because multiplying both sides by $a^{-1}$ gives $a = e$. An element of order 2 cannot be the identity.</p></li><li><p data-path-to-node=\"10,1,0\"><b data-index-in-node=\"0\" data-path-to-node=\"10,1,0\">$a^2 = e$</b>: This matches the definition of an element of order 2. It implies $a \\cdot a = e$, which also means $a = a^{-1}$ (the element is its own inverse).</p></li><li><p data-path-to-node=\"10,2,0\"><b data-index-in-node=\"0\" data-path-to-node=\"10,2,0\">$a = e$</b>: This describes an element of <b data-index-in-node=\"36\" data-path-to-node=\"10,2,0\">order 1</b>. By definition, the identity is the only element in a group with order 1</p></li><li><p data-path-to-node=\"10,2,0\">so option B is correct.</p></li></ul></div><br><br><small>Source: <a href=\"https://gateoverflow.in/528970/go-classes-dpp-gate-cs-discrete-mathematics-group-theory\" target=\"_blank\">GATE Overflow #528970</a></small></div>"
        }
    ]
});

